tel 1.0

base zeppelin
import soundness

info   ZIP archives and JARs as immutable values: typed entry paths, streamed content, scoped reading
detail
    _Zeppelin_ reads and writes ZIP archives, and the JAR files which are ZIP archives with a
    manifest, without the mutable random-access objects of `java.util.zip`. A `Zipfile` is an
    immutable list of `Zip.Entry`s, each with a validated archive-relative path — a `Path on
    Zip`, which cannot be absolute or climb above the archive's root, so an entry name such as
    `../../etc/passwd` is rejected rather than extracted — and content which is produced as a
    stream when it is read. An archive serializes as a stream of bytes, so writing one is
    writing any other value, and reading one inflates each entry incrementally, in bounded
    chunks, only when that entry is read.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import filesystemOptions.{createNonexistentParents, overwritePreexisting}
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory
    import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

    val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "hello.zip"
    val entry = Zip.Entry(t"docs/hello.txt".as[Path on Zip], t"Hello world".in[Data])
    path.write(Zipfile(List(entry)))

    Zipfile.read(path).entry(t"docs/hello.txt".as[Path on Zip]).read[Text]
    ```

    An archive is used in one of two ways. `Zipfile.read` gives a detached `Zipfile`, whose
    entries re-open the file for each read and so stay usable anywhere. `path.open[Zip]()` (or
    `data.open[Zip]()`) instead opens a scope in which `zipfile` is a `Zip.Handle` reading
    through one view of the file held open for the block, so entries must be consumed within
    it; `path.open[Jar]()` gives a `Jar.JarHandle`, the same handle with the parsed manifest.
    `directory.archive[Zip]()` archives a whole directory tree, deterministically.

    An entry read from an archive keeps every header field around its content, so writing it
    back reproduces the original byte for byte, the ZIP64 records excepted, which are rebuilt;
    entries can be aligned for memory-mapping, as Android's `zipalign` does; an archive may carry
    a `prefix` of leading bytes, which is how a self-extracting or directly executable archive
    is made; and archives beyond the 4 GiB and 65,535-entry limits of the original format are
    written and read with ZIP64 records.

    Compression is a contextual `Zip.Compression` policy: deflation by default, through
    pneumatic's pure-Scala deflater, falling back to storing whenever deflation would not make
    an entry smaller. Every failure is a `Zip.Error` whose `Zip.Error.Reason` says what was
    wrong.

    The library has one source set, `zeppelin.core`, which compiles wherever galilei's
    `FilesystemBackend` does, since every file is read through the backend in scope. Archives
    open read-only for now: a `Write` mode is refused. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella.

refer zeppelin.Zipfile
refer zeppelin.Zip
refer zeppelin.Jar
refer zeppelin.zipfile

entry .Zip   the ZIP form and plane: entries, compression, errors and the scoped archive handle
  detail
      `Zip` is two things which share a name. As a **type**, it is the archive's _form_, named
      in `path.open[Zip]()` and `directory.archive[Zip]()`, and the **plane** of paths within an
      archive: an entry's path is a `Path on Zip`, `/`-separated and relative to the archive's
      root, each segment held to `.Rules`. As an **object**, it holds the types every archive
      uses — `Entry`, `Compression`, `Method`, `Error`, `Event` and `Handle` — and the givens
      which make the form and the plane work with no import.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val ref: Path on Zip = t"META-INF/services/app.Plugin".as[Path on Zip]

      val archive: Data =
        Zipfile(List(Zip.Entry(ref, t"app.DefaultPlugin".in[Data]))).serialize.read[Data]

      archive.open[Zip]():
        zipfile.entry(ref).read[Text]
      ```

      A path on the `Zip` plane encodes without a leading `/`, since ZIP entry names are
      relative and `java -jar` rejects an absolute one; a text which names an absolute path,
      climbs with `..`, or holds a character that Windows or the format forbids does not decode.
  refer zeppelin.Zipfile
  refer zeppelin.Zip.Entry
  refer zeppelin.Zip.Handle
  entry .Rules   what a segment of a path within a ZIP archive may be
      A segment must not contain `\`, `"`, `/`, `:`, `*`, `?`, `<`, `>` or `|`, and must not be
      `.` or `..`. The rules are applied to every entry name an archive is read with, so a
      traversing (Zip Slip) name raises `Zip.Error` with reason `InvalidName` rather than
      producing a path which escapes the archive.
  entry .compliant   says that a Linux path segment is a valid ZIP entry segment
      The `Linux is Compliant on Zip` given, which lets a path on a Linux filesystem be
      reinterpreted as one within an archive.
  entry .compliant2   says that a macOS path segment is a valid ZIP entry segment
      The `MacOs is Compliant on Zip` given, as `.compliant` is for Linux.
  entry .nominative   the given which holds ZIP path segments to `.Rules`
  entry .submissible   accepts the root `%` of any path moved onto the ZIP plane with `path.on[Zip]`
      A path's root is checked when the path is moved to another plane; an archive's root is
      implicit, so the check always passes.
  entry .filesystem   the ZIP plane as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, with no unique root
      Named `ZIP`, with `/` as its separator and `.` and `..` as the self and parent segments.
  entry .radical   writes the archive root as the empty text, so a path encodes as `a/b.txt`
  entry .eradicable   reads the archive root from text, which cannot fail
      An archive's root is implicit in every entry's name, so it occupies no characters and any
      text decodes to the root `%`.
  entry .openable   opens an archive at a path as `Zip`: `path.open[Zip]()`
      The `Openable` given for any path type on a plane with a `FilesystemBackend` in scope. It
      needs that backend, a `Tactic[Zip.Error]` and a `Tactic[Io.Error]`, and is in the
      companion of `Zip`, so it needs no import. Its result is a `Zip.Handle`, reached in the
      block as `zipfile`.
  entry .dataOpenable   opens an in-memory archive as `Zip`: `data.open[Zip]()`
      The `Openable` given for `Data`, needing a `Tactic[Zip.Error]`. No channel is involved,
      but access is scoped all the same, for consistency with an archive opened from a path.
  entry .archivable   archives a directory tree as a `Zipfile`: `directory.archive[Zip]()`
      In the companion of `Zip`, so it needs no import. It needs a `FilesystemBackend`, a
      `Tactic[Io.Error]`, a `Tactic[Zip.Error]` and a `Zip.Compression` (the default deflation
      is in implicit scope). See `Zip.Archivable` for what is archived.
  entry .Archivable   the archiver behind `directory.archive[Zip]()`: every descendant, in a fixed order
    detail
        Every descendant of the directory becomes an entry — a directory as a directory entry, a
        file with its contents and modification time — in pre-order, each directory's children
        sorted in codepoint order, so the same tree always yields the same archive, byte for
        byte. ZIP as zeppelin writes it has no symbolic links, so links are followed. The
        contextual `Zip.Compression` applies unless one is passed as a flag.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

        val tree: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]

        tree.archive[Zip]()
        tree.archive[Zip](Zip.Compression.Stored)
        ```

        A name which cannot be a `Path on Zip` raises `Zip.Error` with reason `InvalidName`,
        and an unreadable file raises `Io.Error`.
    entry #archive   archives the tree beneath a path, with an optional `Zip.Compression` flag
  entry .Method   the compression method recorded on an entry: `Stored` or `Deflate`
    detail
        What an entry's bytes actually are in the archive, as opposed to the `Zip.Compression`
        policy asked for: an entry whose deflated form would be no smaller than its content is
        `Stored` whatever the policy. An archive using any other method raises `Zip.Error` with
        reason `UnsupportedMethod` when it is read.
    entry #id   the method's number in a ZIP header: `0` for `Stored`, `8` for `Deflate`
  entry .Compression   the policy for compressing new entries: store them, or deflate them
    detail
        Consulted, as a contextual value, when a `Zip.Entry` is made from raw content, and
        passed as a flag to `directory.archive[Zip]`. `Stored` keeps every entry's bytes as they
        are; `Deflate(level)` deflates, falling back to storing any entry which deflation would
        not make smaller. Deflation is pneumatic's, which has a single compression level, so
        `Deflate(0)` stores, as zlib's level 0 would, and every other level deflates alike.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label The default policy deflates
          code
              import charsets.utf8Charset

              Zip.Entry(t"log.txt".as[Path on Zip], (t"tick "*1000).in[Data]).method
              // Zip.Method.Deflate
        frame
          label A stored policy keeps the bytes as they are
          code
              import charsets.utf8Charset

              locally:
                given Zip.Compression = Zip.Compression.Stored
                Zip.Entry(t"log.txt".as[Path on Zip], (t"tick "*1000).in[Data]).method
                // Zip.Method.Stored
        frame
          label Deflating at level 0 stores too, as zlib's level 0 would
          code
              import charsets.utf8Charset

              locally:
                given Zip.Compression = Zip.Compression.Deflate(0)
                Zip.Entry(t"log.txt".as[Path on Zip], (t"tick "*1000).in[Data]).method
                // Zip.Method.Stored
        ##
        ```
    entry .default   the default policy, `Deflate(-1)`, found in implicit scope with no import
    entry .Deflate   deflates each entry, unless deflation would not make it smaller
      entry #level   the requested level; `0` stores, and every other level deflates alike
  entry .Entry   one entry of a ZIP archive: its path, its compressed content and its header fields
    detail
        An immutable value: an archive-relative `#ref`, the `#method` its bytes are stored with,
        their CRC-32 and sizes, a producer of the stored bytes, a DOS timestamp, and every
        other header field an archive can carry. An entry is made from any content which is
        `Streamable by Data`, compressed once under the contextual `Zip.Compression`, and it
        reads, inflating incrementally, as any type through the `.streamable` given.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        val ref: Path on Zip = t"notes/today.txt".as[Path on Zip]
        val entry: Zip.Entry = Zip.Entry(ref, t"Hello world".in[Data])
        val stamped: Zip.Entry = Zip.Entry.at(ref, t"Hello world".in[Data], 1709214330000L)

        entry.read[Text]
        (entry.method, entry.uncompressedSize, entry.compressedSize)
        ```

        The header fields after `#alignment` exist so that an archive read apart can be written
        back byte for byte: `Unset` means the value zeppelin derives itself (the UTF-8 flag
        exactly when the name needs it; version 20, or 45 for a ZIP64 entry; external attribute
        `0x10` for a directory and 0 otherwise; no extra field), and a value is written verbatim.
        They are changed with `#withHeaders`, the alignment with `#aligned` and the directory
        flag with `#asDirectory`: the case class's own `copy` cannot be called from
        capture-checked code, since it would pass the `#storedBytes` producer through a pure
        parameter.
    refer zeppelin.Zip.Compression
    refer zeppelin.Zipfile
    entry .apply   an entry made from any streamable content, compressed under the policy in scope
        `Zip.Entry(ref, content)` reads the content once, computes its CRC-32, and stores or
        deflates it as the contextual `Zip.Compression` says, with the DOS epoch (1 January
        1980) as its timestamp. The case class's full constructor, which takes every field
        explicitly, is the same name.
    entry .at   an entry made from content, with a modification time given as any instant type
        As `.apply`, with the time recorded in the entry's DOS date and time fields, in UTC,
        since a ZIP timestamp records no zone. The instant may be any type `Abstractable
        across Instants to Long`; an instant before 1980, which DOS cannot represent, is recorded
        as the first second of 1980, and DOS times are stored to two-second precision.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Zip.Entry.at(t"a.txt".as[Path on Zip], t"x".in[Data], 1709214330000L)
        ```
    entry .streamable   reads an entry's decompressed content as bytes, or as any decodable type
        The `Streamable by Data over Credit` given, so an entry can be `read[Text]`, `read[Data]`
        or read as anything decodable from a stream; each read is a fresh stream of `#contents`.
    entry #ref   the entry's path within the archive
    entry #method   the compression method its bytes are stored with, as a `Zip.Method`
    entry #crc32   the CRC-32 of the uncompressed content
    entry #uncompressedSize   the size of the content, in bytes
    entry #compressedSize   the size of the bytes stored in the archive
    entry #storedBytes   a producer of the stored (possibly compressed) bytes, called once per read
        Called afresh for each read, so an entry read from a detached `Zipfile` re-opens its
        source each time and stays reusable. `#contents` is the decompressed form.
    entry #dosTime   the modification time, in the packed DOS time format
    entry #dosDate   the modification date, in the packed DOS date format
    entry #directory   whether the entry is a directory, written with a trailing `/` on its name
    entry #comment   the entry's comment, or `Unset`
    entry #alignment   the byte multiple at which the entry's data must begin; `1` for none
    entry #flags   the general-purpose bit flags, or `Unset` for those zeppelin derives
        Bit 3 marks the entry as streamed (`#streamed`): its CRC and sizes are written in a data
        descriptor after the payload, which is how an archive is written to a sink that cannot
        seek. Bit 11, that the name is UTF-8, is set by default exactly when the name needs it.
    entry #versionMadeBy   the central header's "version made by", or `Unset` for zeppelin's own
    entry #localVersion   the local header's "version needed to extract", or `Unset` for zeppelin's own
    entry #centralVersion   the central header's "version needed to extract", or `Unset` for zeppelin's own
    entry #internalAttributes   the internal file attributes, or `Unset` for none
    entry #externalAttributes   the external file attributes, where a Unix mode lives, or `Unset` for the default
        The default is `0x10`, the DOS directory attribute, for a directory, and 0 otherwise. A
        Unix mode is held in the upper sixteen bits.
    entry #localExtra   the local header's extra field, without its ZIP64 record, or `Unset`
    entry #centralExtra   the central header's extra field, without its ZIP64 record, or `Unset`
        The ZIP64 record is left out of both extra fields because the writer reconstructs it from
        the entry's sizes and offset.
    entry #localSizes   whether a streamed entry's local header repeats its CRC and sizes
        Writers differ: some repeat them, some leave them zero for the data descriptor alone.
        Ignored for an entry which is not streamed.
    entry #streamed   whether a data descriptor follows the entry's payload, by bit 3 of `#flags`
    entry #contents   the entry's decompressed content, as a fresh stream per call
        A `Deflate` entry is inflated incrementally, so a payload of any size, including one
        beyond 2 GiB, is read, inflated and consumed in bounded chunks.
    entry #aligned   the same entry, its data required to begin at a multiple of a byte offset
        Achieved by padding the local header's extra field with zero bytes, as `zipalign` does.
        It is meaningful only for a stored entry, whose bytes a reader may memory-map directly:
        an aligned `classes.dex` or `resources.arsc`, or a page-aligned (`4096`) native library,
        needs no copy.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        given Zip.Compression = Zip.Compression.Stored

        Zip.Entry(t"lib/libapp.so".as[Path on Zip], t"payload".in[Data]).aligned(4096)
        ```
    entry #asDirectory   the same entry, recorded as a directory
        Its name serializes with a trailing `/` and the DOS directory attribute is set.
    entry #withHeaders   the same entry with some of its header fields replaced
        Each parameter — `flags`, `versionMadeBy`, `localVersion`, `centralVersion`,
        `internalAttributes`, `externalAttributes`, `localExtra`, `centralExtra` and
        `localSizes` — defaults to the entry's present value, so a call names only the fields it
        sets.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        val entry = Zip.Entry(t"run.sh".as[Path on Zip], t"echo hi".in[Data])
        entry.withHeaders(externalAttributes = 0x81ed0000L, versionMadeBy = 0x031e)
        ```
  entry .Error   a failure to read, write or open a ZIP archive, with the reason
    detail
        Error 751: _the ZIP operation failed because `reason`_, with the
        `Zip.Error.Reason`'s number as its subcode. Raised by `Zipfile.read`, by
        `Zipfile#entry`, by serializing a `Zipfile`, by opening an archive as `Zip` or `Jar`, and
        by `Zipfile.rebase`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        capture[Zip.Error](Zipfile.read(t"not a zip file".in[Data])).reason
        // Zip.Error.Reason.MissingEocd
        ```
    entry .Reason   what was wrong with a ZIP archive or operation, each with a stable number
      detail
          Each case has a stable number:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `DuplicateEntry(path)`  1  _the path `path` is a duplicate entry_  refused before any byte is serialized
          row  `NotFound(path)`  2  _path `path` was not found in the ZIP file_
          row  `InvalidName(name)`  3  _the name `name` is not valid for a ZIP entry_  not a valid `Path on Zip`
          row  `UnsupportedMethod(method)`  4  _the compression method `method` is not supported_  anything but stored or deflate
          row  `MissingEocd`  5  _no end-of-central-directory record could be found_  so the bytes are not a ZIP archive
          row  `TruncatedArchive`  6  _the ZIP archive ended unexpectedly_
          row  `BadSignature(expected)`  7  _an expected record signature (`expected`) was absent_
          row  `Zip64Error`  8  _the ZIP64 metadata could not be interpreted_
          row  `WriteUnsupported`  9  _ZIP archives cannot yet be opened for writing_  an archive opened in a mode with `Write`
          row  `AlreadyExists`  10  _an archive already exists at this path_
          row  `CannotWrite(detail)`  11  _the archive could not be written: `detail`_
          ```
      entry .DuplicateEntry   two entries in one archive share a path
        entry #path   the duplicated path
      entry .NotFound   the archive has no entry at the path asked for
        entry #path   the path which was asked for
      entry .InvalidName   an entry name is not a valid path within an archive
        entry #name   the name, as the archive spelled it
      entry .UnsupportedMethod   an entry uses a compression method other than stored or deflate
        entry #method   the method's number, from the entry's header
      entry .BadSignature   a record's signature is not the one expected at that position
        entry #expected   the signature that was expected
      entry .CannotWrite   an archive could not be written
        entry #detail   what went wrong
      entry #number   the reason's subcode, from 1 to 11
    entry .communicable   renders each `Zip.Error.Reason` as a sentence, such as _the ZIP archive ended unexpectedly_
    entry #reason   what was wrong, as a `Zip.Error.Reason`
  entry .Event   what reading a ZIP archive logs
    detail
        `Zipfile.read` from a path logs `Read` at `info` level, tagged `Log.Serialization`. A
        `Zip.Event is Loggable` must be in scope for that read; `logging.silentLogging`
        discards it.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
    entry .Read   an archive was read: _read `entries` entries from the zip archive `path`_
      entry #path   the archive's path, as text
      entry #entries   the number of entries it held
  entry .Handle   an archive opened for the duration of a block, reached as `zipfile`
    detail
        The scoped handle `path.open[Zip]()` and `data.open[Zip]()` provide, which `zipfile`
        names inside the block. Unlike a detached `Zipfile`, whose entries re-open the file for
        every read, a `Zip.Handle` reads through one view of the file, held open by the
        filesystem backend for the duration of the block, so entries are read with no per-read
        open and close — and, correspondingly, must be consumed within the block.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        val archive: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/app.zip".as[Path on Linux]

        archive.open[Zip]():
          zipfile.entries.map(_.ref.encode)
          zipfile.entry(t"README".as[Path on Zip]).read[Text]
        ```

        It is a capability, so for callers compiled with capture checking the handle and the
        entries it yields are confined to the block.
    refer zeppelin.zipfile
    refer zeppelin.Jar.JarHandle
    entry #entries   the archive's entries, in the order of its central directory
    entry #entry   the entry at a path, raising `Zip.Error` with reason `NotFound` if there is none
    entry #comment   the archive's comment, or `Unset`
  entry .Openable   the opener behind `path.open[Zip]()`, for a path on any plane with a filesystem backend
    detail
        Built by the `Zip.openable` given; a caller never names the class. A mode with `Write`
        raises `Zip.Error` with reason `WriteUnsupported`, since archives open read-only for now.
    entry #open   parses the archive through the backend's view of the file, and runs the block
  entry .DataOpenable   the opener behind `data.open[Zip]()`, for an archive already in memory
    detail
        Built by the `Zip.dataOpenable` given. A mode with `Write` raises `Zip.Error` with reason
        `WriteUnsupported`.
    entry #open   parses the archive from the bytes, and runs the block with its handle

entry .Zipfile   a ZIP archive as an immutable value: its entries, comment and leading bytes
  detail
      A `Zipfile` is the whole archive in hand: a `List` of `Zip.Entry`s, an optional archive
      comment, and an optional `prefix` of raw bytes to place before the archive proper. It is
      made directly from entries, read from a path or from bytes with `.read`, or produced by
      `directory.archive[Zip]()`, and it serializes as a stream, so it is written to any
      destination with `write`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import filesystemOptions.{createNonexistentParents, overwritePreexisting}
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      val path: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "notes.zip"
      val entries = List(Zip.Entry(t"a.txt".as[Path on Zip], t"alpha".in[Data]))

      path.write(Zipfile(entries, comment = t"generated"))

      val notes: Zipfile = Zipfile.read(path)
      notes.entries.map(_.ref.encode)
      notes.entry(t"a.txt".as[Path on Zip]).read[Text]
      ```

      The entries of a `Zipfile` read from a path are detached: each read re-opens the file
      through the backend, so the value is usable anywhere, at the cost of an open per read.
      `path.open[Zip]()` reads through one open view instead, for the duration of a block.

      A ZIP reader locates the archive's directory from the end of the file rather than the
      start, and every offset it records is relative, so an archive concatenated onto arbitrary
      leading bytes still reads. That is what the `prefix` is for: a self-extracting or directly
      executable archive is a launcher followed by a ZIP. Reading an archive with leading bytes
      gives them back as its `prefix`.
  refer zeppelin.Zip.Entry
  refer zeppelin.Zip.Handle
  entry .read   reads an archive from a path, or from bytes in memory
      `Zipfile.read(path)` reads the central directory through the `FilesystemBackend` in scope,
      needing a `Tactic[Io.Error]` and a `Tactic[Zip.Error]`, and logs `Zip.Event.Read`, so a
      `Zip.Event is Loggable` must be in scope too. `Zipfile.read(data)` reads from bytes and
      logs nothing. Only the directory and each entry's local header are read here; payloads are
      read as entries are. Raises `Zip.Error` with reason `MissingEocd` for bytes which are not
      a ZIP archive, and `InvalidName` for an entry name which would escape the archive.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      capture[Zip.Error](Zipfile.read(t"this is not a zip file".in[Data])).reason
      ```
  entry .rebase   shifts a ZIP64 archive's one physical offset, after bytes are prepended to it
      Every offset a ZIP archive records is relative to its central directory, with one
      exception: the ZIP64 locator's pointer to the ZIP64 end-of-central-directory record is a
      physical offset in the file. Prepending a prefix to an archive by concatenation, or
      cutting bytes from one, invalidates exactly that field. Zeppelin's reader tolerates it,
      but the JDK's follows the pointer and then declines to open the archive, with no
      diagnostic. `Zipfile.rebase(path, delta)` shifts the pointer by `delta` bytes, in place,
      through the `FilesystemBackend` in scope; an archive with no ZIP64 locator — anything of
      fewer than 65,535 entries and 4 GiB, which is nearly everything — is left untouched. A
      pointer which would leave the file raises `Zip.Error` with reason `Zip64Error`.

      A `Zipfile` written with a `prefix` needs no rebasing, since its offsets are computed
      with the prefix in place.
  entry .streamable   serializes a `Zipfile` as a stream of bytes, so it can be written anywhere
      The `Streamable by Data over Credit` given, which needs a `Tactic[Zip.Error]` since
      serializing refuses duplicate entry names. It is in the companion of `Zipfile`, so
      `path.write(zipfile)` needs no import.
  entry #entries   the archive's entries, in the order they are written
  entry #comment   the archive's comment, or `Unset`
  entry #prefix   raw bytes placed before the archive proper, or `Unset`
  entry #entry   the entry at a path, raising `Zip.Error` with reason `NotFound` if there is none
  entry #serialize   the archive as a lazy stream of bytes, its entries' payloads opened in turn
      Raises `Zip.Error` with reason `DuplicateEntry` before any bytes are produced if two
      entries share a path, since no reader could resolve them. The prefix comes first, then each
      entry's local header, payload and data descriptor — each payload opened only when the
      stream reaches it, and drained in bounded chunks — then the central directory and the end
      records, with ZIP64 records wherever an entry, an offset or the entry count overflows the
      original format.

entry .Jar   the form for Java archives: `path.open[Jar]()` gives a ZIP handle with the parsed manifest
  detail
      A JAR is a ZIP, so opening one as `Jar` gives a `Jar.JarHandle`, a `Zip.Handle` refined
      with the main attributes of `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`; entries and their content behave
      exactly as they do for the `Zip` form. A JAR is written as any other `Zipfile`, with the
      entry `Jar.manifest` makes first.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import filesystemOptions.{createNonexistentParents, overwritePreexisting}
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      val manifest = Jar.manifest(t"Manifest-Version" -> t"1.0", t"Main-Class" -> t"app.Main")
      val jar: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "app.jar"
      jar.write(Zipfile(List(manifest)))

      jar.open[Jar]():
        zipfile.manifest
      ```
  refer zeppelin.Zip.Handle
  refer zeppelin.Zipfile
  entry .manifest   the `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF` entry for a list of main attributes
      Each attribute is written as `Key: value`, with lines wrapped at the JAR specification's 72
      bytes by continuation lines beginning with a space, `\r\n` line endings, and a blank line
      ending the main section. The entry is compressed under the `Zip.Compression` in scope, and
      convention prefers it first in the `Zipfile`.
  entry .JarHandle   an opened JAR: a `Zip.Handle` with the main attributes of its manifest
    detail
        What `zipfile` names inside a `path.open[Jar]()` or `data.open[Jar]()` block. Its
        entries, and reading them, are those of a `Zip.Handle`.
    entry #manifest   the manifest's main attributes, or an empty map if the archive has none
        Read from `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`: continuation lines (beginning with a space) rejoin the
        attribute before them, as the specification requires, and parsing stops at the first
        blank line, which ends the main section, so per-entry sections are not included.
  entry .JarOpenable   the opener behind `path.open[Jar]()`, for a path on any plane with a filesystem backend
    detail
        Built by the `Jar.openable` given. A mode with `Write` raises `Zip.Error` with reason
        `WriteUnsupported`.
    entry #open   parses the archive through the backend's view of the file, and runs the block
  entry .JarDataOpenable   the opener behind `data.open[Jar]()`, for a JAR already in memory
    detail
        Built by the `Jar.dataOpenable` given.
    entry #open   parses the archive from the bytes, and runs the block with its handle
  entry .openable   opens a JAR at a path as `Jar`: `path.open[Jar]()`
      Needs a `FilesystemBackend` for the path's plane, a `Tactic[Zip.Error]` and a
      `Tactic[Io.Error]`. In the companion of `Jar`, so it needs no import.
  entry .dataOpenable   opens an in-memory JAR as `Jar`: `data.open[Jar]()`
      Needs a `Tactic[Zip.Error]`. In the companion of `Jar`, so it needs no import.

entry .zipfile   the archive handle in scope, inside a `path.open[Zip]()` or `open[Jar]` block
    Names the contextual `Zip.Handle`, as galilei's `file` names an open file. It is
    transparent, so its type is the handle's own precise type: a `Jar.JarHandle`, with
    `manifest`, inside an `open[Jar]` block.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import denominative.dysasymptotics.linearSize

    val archive: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/app.zip".as[Path on Linux]

    archive.open[Zip]():
      zipfile.entries.size
    ```
